      "examAnswer": "Quantifiers are used to express the extent to which a predicate is true over a domain. The universal quantifier, denoted ∀, when applied to a predicate P(x) gives the statement ∀x P(x), read 'for all x, P(x)', which is true if and only if P(x) is true for every element x of the domain; a single counter-example is sufficient to establish its falsity. The existential quantifier, denoted ∃, gives the statement ∃x P(x), read 'there exists an x such that P(x)', which is true if and only if P(x) is true for at least one element of the domain. The negations of quantified statements are governed by the laws ¬∀x P(x) ≡ ∃x ¬P(x) and ¬∃x P(x) ≡ ∀x ¬P(x), so that in negating a quantified statement the quantifier is interchanged and the predicate is negated. The truth value of a quantified statement always depends on the domain of discourse.",
